# Errors

Karbone never throws for expected failure modes. Every SDK method is declared as
`context(_: Raise<KarboneError>) suspend fun ...`, using [Arrow](https://arrow-kt.io/)'s `Raise` to make failure part of the
return type instead of an exception.

Every `KarboneError.Api` also exposes `code`, Carbone's own error code when the response carries one. For example a *test* API key
may only produce PDF output: asking for DOCX yields `Unexpected(status=200, code="w117", message="With a test key, you can only convert in the following format (pdf)")`.

## The `KarboneError` hierarchy

| Type                       | When                                                                                   | Key fields                          |
|----------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------|
| `Unauthorized`             | HTTP 401, or a malformed on-premise JWT (see below)                                     | `status`, `message`, `code`          |
| `TemplateNotFound`         | HTTP 404 on a call addressing a known template id                                       | `id`, `status`, `message`            |
| `RenderNotFound`           | HTTP 404 on `renders.download(id)`                                                       | `id`, `status`, `message`            |
| `PayloadTooLarge`          | HTTP 413                                                                                 | `status`, `message`                  |
| `UnsupportedTemplateFormat`| HTTP 415                                                                                 | `status`, `message`                  |
| `BadRequest`               | HTTP 400 or 422 — rejected metadata, invalid JSON, missing field, empty template...      | `status`, `message`                  |
| `RenderFailed`             | HTTP 500 on a render endpoint                                                            | `status`, `message`                  |
| `Unexpected`               | Any other non-2xx status, or a 2xx response with `success: false`                        | `status`, `message`, `body`          |
| `InvalidRequest`           | A request rejected client-side before any call (e.g. `batch` without `startAsync`)       | `reason`                              |
| `Transport`                | Connection, timeout or I/O failure                                                       | `cause`                               |
| `Serialization`            | A response body that could not be parsed                                                | `cause`, `body`                       |
| `TemplateRead`             | Local template content could not be read                                                | `source`, `cause`                     |

`Unauthorized`, `TemplateNotFound`, `RenderNotFound`, `PayloadTooLarge`, `UnsupportedTemplateFormat`, `BadRequest`,
`RenderFailed` and `Unexpected` all implement `KarboneError.Api`, which exposes `status` and `message` uniformly. **`message`
is always the raw `error` text from the Carbone response, never rewritten or translated** — safe to log or surface as-is.

Every `KarboneError` has a `describe(): String` for a human-readable summary, useful in logs.

## Handling errors with `Raise`

The idiomatic way to call Karbone is inside a `Raise<KarboneError>` scope, most simply Arrow's `either`:

```kotlin
import arrow.core.raise.either

val result = either { karbone.renders.render(template, data) } // Either<KarboneError, RenderedDocument>

result.fold(
  { error -> log.warn { "Render failed: ${error.describe()}" } },
  { document -> saveToDisk(document) },
)
```

`recover` lets you fall back to a value instead of propagating:

```kotlin
import arrow.core.raise.recover

suspend fun statusOrUnknown(karbone: Karbone): ApiStatus = recover({ karbone.status() }) { error ->
  ApiStatus(success = false, code = null, message = error.describe(), version = null)
}
```

`fold` runs matching success/failure handlers directly, without materializing an `Either`:

```kotlin
import arrow.core.raise.fold

fold(
  block = { karbone.templates.delete(id) },
  recover = { error -> log.warn { error.describe() } },
  transform = { log.info { "Deleted $id" } },
)
```

Because `KarboneError` is `sealed`, you can also `when` on it exhaustively wherever that reads better than `Either` combinators.

## The blocking facade

`Karbone.blocking()` wraps every call with `either { ... }.fold(...)`, throwing `KarboneException` on the `Left` side:

```kotlin
public class KarboneException(public val error: KarboneError) : RuntimeException(...)
```

`error` is the same `KarboneError` value that `Raise` would have raised; `getMessage()` is `error.describe()`. This is the
facade to reach for from Java, or from any Kotlin code that has not adopted `Raise`.

## Malformed on-premise tokens

`carbone-ee` answers a malformed bearer token with **HTTP 500** and an error message containing `"Invalid JSON Web Token"`,
rather than a 401. Karbone detects that message and maps it to `KarboneError.Unauthorized` regardless of the HTTP status, so
callers get a consistent `Unauthorized` for every authentication failure — missing token, forged token, or expired token —
whatever status code the server happens to answer with.
